A stream is aflow of data btn a source and a destination

In Java, I/O means Input/Output:
- Input means receiving or reading data.
- Output means sending or writing data.

For example, imagine that you have a text file called student.txt containing:
        Name: Eric
        Course: Software Engineering
        Age: 22


If your Java program wants to read this information, the data flows from the file into the program.

    Reading a file (Input)

        student.txt // this is the source of data 
     
        Input stream // for reading and receiving data

        Java program // Recevies and process that data

Now imagine that your java prograam wants to save informatikon to another file, so the data flows out of the progrma and into the file 

          Writing a file (Output)

                Java program //  Produces the data

                Output stream  // this writes that data 

                 output.txt //Destination for the data


////   Why does Java use I/O streams? ///

== to interact with different data sources and destinations
== Read data from files.
== Write data to files.
== Receive input from the keyboard.
== Display the output on the console 
== Exchange data with network connections 
== Process data efficientluy without necessarily loading an entire file into the memory 

Notes : reading a large file one character at a time or in manageable blocks can be more practical than loading the entire file into memory.

